The '''[[2000–01 NBA season|2000]] [[NBA draft]]''' was held on June 28, 2000, at the [[Target Center]] in [[Minneapolis]]. It was the last draft held at the home arena of an NBA team until [[2011 NBA draft|2011]]; the following and subsequent drafts (through 2010) all took place at [[The Theater at Madison Square Garden]] in [[New York City]] (though Madison Square Garden itself is the home of the [[New York Knicks]], they do not play in the theater). In addition, is the last draft held outside of New York City. As of [[2025 NBA draft|2025]], it is also the last NBA draft where a college senior was the number-one overall selection, and, along with the [[1951 NBA Draft]], one of only two (and the most recent) drafts with no [[Naismith Memorial Basketball Hall of Fame]] players (excluding drafts with players not yet eligible).

The 2000 draft class is considered the worst in NBA history.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Martin |first=Josh |title=Remembering the Hauntingly Terrible 2000 NBA Draft Class |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2490422-remembering-the-hauntingly-terrible-2000-nba-draft-class |access-date=2025-04-15 |website=bleacherreport.com |language=en}}</ref> Few of its draftees would enjoy extended careers in the league. Just three of them—top pick [[Kenyon Martin]], first-round selection [[Jamaal Magloire]] (19th overall) and second-round pick [[Michael Redd]] (43rd overall) -- ever played in an NBA All-Star Game. Each of the three made their lone All-Star appearance in [[2004 NBA All-Star Game|2004]]. The three cumulative All-Star appearances marks the lowest cumulative appearances since the [[1952 NBA draft]]. Redd was the lone player from this draft to ever be chosen for an [[All-NBA Team]] (his sole appearance was on the third team in [[2003–04 NBA season|2004]]). Only three players in this draft class won a major end-of-season award in their careers: [[Hedo Türkoğlu]] was named [[NBA Most Improved Player Award|Most Improved Player]] in [[2007–08 NBA season|2008]], [[Mike Miller (basketball, born 1980)|Mike Miller]] won the [[NBA Rookie of the Year]] and [[NBA Sixth Man of the Year]] awards in 2001 and 2006 respectively, and [[Jamal Crawford]] was awarded the [[NBA Sixth Man of the Year]] three times in 2010, 2014 and 2016.

''[[Sports Illustrated]]'' named this entire draft class (as opposed to individual players) the sixth biggest bust of the modern era – making it the only draft class among the site's top 20 list.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/multimedia/photo_gallery/2005/06/24/gallery.nbabusts/content.15.html |work=CNN |title=SI.com – Photo Gallery – NBA Draft Busts |access-date=28 March 2007  |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070418103854/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/multimedia/photo_gallery/2005/06/24/gallery.nbabusts/content.15.html |archive-date=18 April 2007 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Just before the [[2009 NBA draft|2009 draft]], ''[[ESPN.com]]'' columnist David Schoenfield graded all of the drafts since the institution of the [[NBA draft lottery|draft lottery]] in [[1985 NBA draft|1985]], and the only draft to which he gave the lowest possible grade of 'F' was the 2000 draft.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/espn/page2/story?page=draftreview/090624|title=The first lottery draft still rates the best |first=David |last=Schoenfield |publisher=ESPN|date=2009-06-25 |access-date=2009-06-26 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090627133325/http://sports.espn.go.com/espn/page2/story?page=draftreview%2F090624 |archive-date=27 June 2009 |url-status=live }}</ref> Using the [[Wins Above Replacement|WARP]] (wins above replacement player) metric, the 2000 NBA [[Draft (sports)|draft]] class collectively produced at a rate of 17.3 wins worse than a group of "average replacement players", effectively making this draft class the only one in [[National Basketball Association|NBA]] history to leave the league's talent pool worse than it had been before.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/10714672/nba-2013-14-rookie-class-worst-ever|title=Is 2013-14 worst rookie class ever?|first=Kevin|last=Pelton|work=ESPN.com|date=2014-04-02|access-date=2014-04-02}}</ref>

Eight of the players selected in this draft never played in an NBA game in their professional basketball careers. Both of the players drafted by the [[San Antonio Spurs]] ([[Chris Carrawell]] and [[Cory Hightower]]) are among this group.<ref>https://www.basketball-reference.com/draft/NBA_2000.html</ref>

The final active player remaining from this class was [[Jamal Crawford]], who retired from the NBA following the [[2019-20 NBA season|2020 season]].

